additional
---------
##1. Edge-Optimized Offline Capabilities
You can design for intermittent connectivity by integrating these:

RAG with LocalStorage: Use llama-index or Haystack to enable a local mini-RAG with stored textbook content and past prompts.

Tiny local models: Deploy a TinyML model (e.g., DistillBERT) for basic Q&A or reading-level classification when offline. It can run inside a mobile device or even on a Raspberry Pi—great for demo impressiveness.
## 3. Data Privacy & Ownership
Open-source storage tools like DuckDB (use for lesson logs locally) or SQLite in the browser (via sql.js) can help keep sensitive teacher/student data offline and locally owned. This reduces parent/community resistance.
##4. Custom Visualization Frameworks
For your visual agents:

Turtle Graphics (e.g., from Python or a JavaScript port) to generate trace-by-hand line drawings.

D3.js or Rough.js for sketchy diagrams that look handmade—emphasizing how a teacher can copy them on a board.

You’ll use Imagen or Gemini-Vision in the demo pipeline, but back it up visually with these so that the same content can be downloaded and used offline.
##5. Open-Source OCR or Layout Parsing
Google Gemini Vision is powerful—but you can improve PDF parsing with:

LayoutParser for extracting structured data from textbook scans (e.g., boxes, question numbers).

Tesseract OCR with Indic models as fallback if Gemini or Vision APIs time out.

